Discovery project: local invertibility of a nonlinear map A rubber sheet is marked with coordinates (u,v). A machine sends each marked point to a new point F(u,v)=(x,y) according to  oxed{x=u+v^2,\qquad y=v+u^2.} This map is close to the identity near the origin, but it folds near another point. The goal of the project is to locate the fold and connect it to the Jacobian determinant. Part 1. The first-order test Compute the Jacobian matrix: J_F(u,v)= egin{pmatrix} x_u \amp x_v\ y_u \amp y_v \end{pmatrix}. Show that  oxed{J_F(u,v)= egin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 2v\ 2u \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}} and  oxed{\det J_F(u,v)=1-4uv.} The inverse function theorem says that F has a local inverse at every point where 1-4uv
e0. So the only possible trouble occurs on the hyperbola uv= rac14. The curve uv= rac14 is where the linear approximation collapses area.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth] \draw[->] (-3.2,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {\(u\)}; \draw[->] (0,-3.2) -- (0,3.3) node[above] {\(v\)}; \draw[domain=0.12:3, samples=120, smooth, thick, blue!60!black] plot (\x,{1/(4*\x)}); \draw[domain=-3:-0.12, samples=120, smooth, thick, blue!60!black] plot (\x,{1/(4*\x)});  ill (0.5,0.5) circle (2.5pt); 
ode[above right] at (0.5,0.5) {\(\left( rac12, rac12ight)\)}; 
ode[blue!60!black] at (2.1,0.8) {\(uv= rac14\)}; 
ode at (0,-3.8) {The determinant vanishes on the fold curve.}; \end{tikzpicture} Part 2. The wedge-product version Use x=u+v^2,\qquad y=v+u^2 to compute dx\qquad	ext{and}\qquad dy. Then show that  oxed{dx\wedge dy=(1-4uv)\,du\wedge dv.} This says that the determinant is not only a matrix number. It is the coefficient that tells how signed area forms transform. Part 3. Near the origin At the origin, J_F(0,0)= egin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}. So near the origin, F(u,v) looks like the identity map. The inverse function theorem gives a local inverse near F(0,0)=(0,0). To first order, u\approx x,\qquad v\approx y. A better second-order approximation can be guessed from the equations x=u+v^2,\qquad y=v+u^2. Since near the origin u\approx x and v\approx y, substitute those first guesses into the squared terms: u\approx x-y^2,\qquad v\approx y-x^2. Check this approximation by substituting u=x-y^2,\qquad v=y-x^2 back into F, and keep only terms up to second degree. Part 4. The fold at \left( rac12, rac12ight) At \left( rac12, rac12ight), the Jacobian matrix is J_F\left( rac12, rac12ight)= egin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1\ 1 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}. This matrix sends (1,-1) to (0,0). So, to first order, motion in the direction (1,-1) is invisible. Now look at this line through the point: (u,v)=\left( rac12+s, rac12-sight). Compute: F\left( rac12+s, rac12-sight) \amp= \left( rac12+s+\left( rac12-sight)^2,\; rac12-s+\left( rac12+sight)^2ight) \amp= \left( rac34+s^2,\; rac34+s^2ight). The same formula holds for -s: F\left( rac12+s, rac12-sight)=F\left( rac12-s, rac12+sight). So every tiny neighborhood of \left( rac12, rac12ight) contains two different points that map to the same output. There is no local inverse there. This is the nonlinear fold that the zero determinant was warning us about. What to hand in Write a short report with the following pieces. The Jacobian matrix and determinant of F. The wedge-product computation dx\wedge dy=(1-4uv)\,du\wedge dv. A drawing of the curve uv= rac14 in the (u,v)-plane. A paragraph explaining why the inverse function theorem applies away from that curve. The exact calculation showing that the two points \left( rac12+s, rac12-sight)\quad	ext{and}\quad\left( rac12-s, rac12+sight) have the same image. A short explanation of how the determinant, the area form, and the visible fold are three views of the same event.

Project: work done in a nonconservative field The field \mathbf F(x,y)=(-y,x) is the simplest rotational field. Its work form is \eta=-y\,dx+x\,dy. It is not conservative, because Q_x-P_y=1-(-1)=2. So a particle can return to where it started and still have nonzero total work done by the field. This project reveals a pattern that is too beautiful to be accidental. A rectangle Let R be the rectangle 0\le x\le a, \qquad 0\le y\le b. Orient its boundary counterclockwise. That means the rectangle stays on your left as you walk around it. Compute \oint_{\partial R} -y\,dx+x\,dy. There are four sides. On the bottom side, y=0,\qquad dy=0, so -y\,dx+x\,dy=0. On the right side, x=a,\qquad dx=0,\qquad 0\le y\le b, so -y\,dx+x\,dy=a\,dy. The contribution is \int_0^b a\,dy=ab. On the top side, y=b,\qquad dy=0, and the path goes from x=a to x=0. The form becomes -b\,dx. The contribution is \int_a^0 -b\,dx=ab. On the left side, x=0,\qquad dx=0, so -y\,dx+x\,dy=0. Adding the four sides,  oxed{ \oint_{\partial R} -y\,dx+x\,dy=2ab. } But ab=\operatorname{Area}(R). So  oxed{ \oint_{\partial R} -y\,dx+x\,dy = 2\,\operatorname{Area}(R). } The rotational field (-y,x) does positive work around a positively oriented rectangle.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, >=stealth] \draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.2,0) node[right] {\(x\)}; \draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,3.8) node[above] {\(y\)}; \draw[fill=gray!8, very thick] (0,0) rectangle (4,2.5); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (0.5,0) -- (1.6,0); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (4,0.6) -- (4,1.6); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (3.2,2.5) -- (2.1,2.5); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (0,1.9) -- (0,0.9); 
ode[below] at (2,0) {\(a\)}; 
ode[left] at (0,1.25) {\(b\)}; 
ode at (2,1.25) {\(R\)};  oreach \x in {0.7, 1.7, 2.7, 3.7} {  oreach \y in {0.5, 1.3, 2.0} { \draw[->, blue!60!black, thick] (\x,\y) -- ++({-0.13*\y},{0.13*\x}); } } 
ode at (2,-0.9) {counterclockwise boundary orientation}; \end{tikzpicture} The coefficient 2 is not a coincidence. It is the scalar curl: Q_x-P_y=2. So the rectangle calculation says  oxed{ \oint_{\partial R}\eta = \iint_R 2\,dA. } The left side is a boundary integral. The right side is an area integral over the region. A circle For a circle of radius R, use \mathbf r(t)=(R\cos t,R\sin t), \qquad 0\le t\le2\pi. Then dx=-R\sin t\,dt, \qquad dy=R\cos t\,dt. So -y\,dx+x\,dy \amp = -(R\sin t)(-R\sin t\,dt) + (R\cos t)(R\cos t\,dt) \amp = R^2\,dt. Thus  oxed{ \oint_C -y\,dx+x\,dy = \int_0^{2\pi}R^2\,dt = 2\pi R^2. } But the area of the disk is \pi R^2. Again,  oxed{ \oint_C -y\,dx+x\,dy = 2\,\operatorname{Area}(	ext{disk}). } A polygon and the shoelace formula Try the same integral around a polygon with vertices (x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\ldots,(x_n,y_n), listed counterclockwise, with (x_{n+1},y_{n+1})=(x_1,y_1). On the straight edge from (x_i,y_i) to (x_{i+1},y_{i+1}), a parametrization is x(t)=x_i+t(x_{i+1}-x_i), y(t)=y_i+t(y_{i+1}-y_i), \qquad 0\le t\le1. Then dx=(x_{i+1}-x_i)\,dt, \qquad dy=(y_{i+1}-y_i)\,dt. Substitution gives -y\,dx+x\,dy =  igl(x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i igr)\,dt. So the edge contribution is x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i. Adding all edges,  oxed{ \oint_{\partial P}-y\,dx+x\,dy = \sum_{i=1}^n  igl(x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i igr). } This suggests the shoelace area formula  oxed{ \operatorname{Area}(P) =  rac12 \sum_{i=1}^n  igl(x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i igr) } for a simple counterclockwise polygon. We will prove it from Green's theorem in . Once established, it gives  oxed{ \oint_{\partial P}-y\,dx+x\,dy = 2\,\operatorname{Area}(P). } The rotational field has become an area-measuring device. By doing work only around the boundary, it finds the area inside. That is the surprise Green's theorem will explain.

Project 1: build and classify quadric surfaces A quadric surface is described by a second-degree equation in x,y,z. In , we learned the main trick:  oxed{ 	ext{To understand a quadric surface, slice it.} } A surface that looks mysterious in three dimensions often becomes clear when we set one variable equal to a constant and study the resulting trace. A seed example: a shifted ellipsoid. Consider  rac{(x-1)^2}{9}+ rac{y^2}{4}+(z+2)^2=1. The shifted terms tell us the center: (1,0,-2). The denominators tell us the semiaxis lengths: 3 	ext{ in the }x	ext{-direction},\qquad 2 	ext{ in the }y	ext{-direction},\qquad 1 	ext{ in the }z	ext{-direction}. So the surface is an ellipsoid, stretched most in the x-direction and least in the z-direction. A few slices confirm this. If z=-2, then  rac{(x-1)^2}{9}+ rac{y^2}{4}=1, an ellipse in the horizontal plane through the center. If y=0, then  rac{(x-1)^2}{9}+(z+2)^2=1, an ellipse in the xz-plane. If x=1, then  rac{y^2}{4}+(z+2)^2=1, an ellipse in the yz-plane. The surface was not guessed from a memorized shape. It was read from its slices. Two traces of the same ellipsoid, drawn in coordinates centered at (1,0,-2). The traces reveal the stretches.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.78, >=stealth]  egin{scope} \draw[->] (-3.6,0) -- (3.8,0) node[below left] {\(x-1\)}; \draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {\(y\)}; \draw[thick] (0,0) ellipse (2.7 and 1.55);  ill (0,0) circle (2pt); 
ode[below] at (0,0) {center}; 
ode at (0,-2.75) {slice \(z=-2\)}; \end{scope}  egin{scope}[shift={(7.4,0)}] \draw[->] (-3.6,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {\(x-1\)}; \draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {\(z+2\)}; \draw[thick] (0,0) ellipse (2.7 and 0.95);  ill (0,0) circle (2pt); 
ode[below] at (0,0) {center}; 
ode at (0,-2.75) {slice \(y=0\)}; \end{scope} \end{tikzpicture} Your task. Create a visual gallery of at least six quadric surfaces. Use six or more of the following:  egin{array}{c} x^2+y^2=4,\[1mm] y=x^2,\[1mm] z=x^2+y^2,\[1mm] z=x^2-y^2,\[1mm] \dfrac{x^2}{9}+\dfrac{y^2}{4}+z^2=1,\[4mm] x^2+y^2-z^2=1,\[1mm] z^2-x^2-y^2=1,\[1mm] z^2=x^2+y^2,\[1mm] \dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{9}+\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1. \end{array} For each surface, include:  egin{array}{ll} 1. & 	ext{the equation,}\ 2. & 	ext{the name of the surface,}\ 3. & 	ext{three traces or cross-sections,}\ 4. & 	ext{a hand sketch, physical model, or computer plot,}\ 5. & 	ext{one sentence explaining how signs, coefficients, or missing variables shape it.} \end{array} Questions to answer. Which surfaces are connected? Which have two separate pieces? Which equations contain a missing variable? What geometric effect does that missing variable have? Which surfaces have horizontal traces that are circles? Which have horizontal traces that are ellipses? Which have hyperbolas? What changes when x^2+y^2-z^2=1 is replaced by z^2-x^2-y^2=1? What changes when an equation is shifted, as in  rac{(x-1)^2}{4}+ rac{(y+2)^2}{9}+ rac{z^2}{16}=1? What to hand in. Write a short illustrated report. It should include a table like this:  egin{array}{c|c|c|c} 	ext{Equation} & 	ext{Name} & 	ext{Useful traces} & 	ext{Geometry note} \ \hline z=x^2+y^2 & 	ext{elliptic paraboloid} & z=c & 	ext{horizontal ellipses grow upward}\[1mm] z=x^2-y^2 & 	ext{hyperbolic paraboloid} & x=0,\ y=0 & 	ext{one slice bends up, one bends down} \end{array} A good gallery should make a reader feel that a quadric equation is not a code to memorize. It is a set of instructions for building a shape.

Project 2: visualize tangent planes and linear approximations gave the tangent plane formula for a differentiable graph z=f(x,y). At the point (a,b,f(a,b)), the tangent plane is  oxed{ z=f(a,b)+f_x(a,b)(x-a)+f_y(a,b)(y-b). } This plane is the best first-order model of the surface near the point. The word near matters. A tangent plane is not a replacement for the whole surface. It is a local microscope. A seed example: a curved roof. Suppose a small roof has height f(x,y)=5+ rac12x- rac14y+ rac18x^2- rac1{16}y^2+ rac18xy. At (a,b)=(2,1), the height is  egin{aligned} f(2,1) &= 5+1- rac14+ rac12- rac1{16}+ rac14\ &=  rac{103}{16}. \end{aligned} The partial derivatives are f_x(x,y)= rac12+ rac14x+ rac18y, and f_y(x,y)=- rac14- rac18y+ rac18x. So f_x(2,1)= rac98, \qquad f_y(2,1)=- rac18. The tangent plane is  oxed{ z= rac{103}{16}+ rac98(x-2)- rac18(y-1). } Now compare the true height with the plane at a nearby point, say (2.2,0.9). The tangent plane predicts L(2.2,0.9) =  rac{103}{16} +  rac98(0.2) -  rac18(-0.1). The actual error is f(2.2,0.9)-L(2.2,0.9). If we move the point twice as close to (2,1), the error should become much smaller. That is what differentiability means: the linear model wins at small scales. A tangent plane is seen in every slice as a tangent line.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82, >=stealth] \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (5.4,0) node[right] {\(x\)}; \draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {\(z\)}; \draw[thick, domain=0.2:4.7, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.3+0.8*\x-0.10*\x*\x}); % Corrected tangent line based on z = 0.28x + 1.976 \draw[blue!60!black, very thick] (1.0,2.256) -- (4.2,3.152); 
ode[blue!60!black, right] at (4.2,3.152) {tangent line in one slice}; % Corrected point coordinates  ill (2.6,2.704) circle (2.5pt); 
ode[above left] at (2.6,2.704) {point}; % Corrected dashed line height \draw[dashed] (2.6,0) -- (2.6,2.704); 
ode at (2.7,-0.8) {one slice of a tangent-plane picture}; \end{tikzpicture} Your task. Choose one smooth surface and one nonsmooth surface. For the smooth surface, use one of these or choose your own:  egin{array}{c} f_1(x,y)=4+x- rac12y- rac14x^2- rac18y^2,\[1mm] f_2(x,y)=5+ rac12x- rac14y+ rac18x^2- rac1{16}y^2+ rac18xy,\[1mm] f_3(x,y)=\sin x\cos y. \end{array} For the nonsmooth surface, use one of these:  egin{array}{c} g_1(x,y)=\sqrt{x^2+y^2},\[1mm] g_2(x,y)=|x|+|y|. \end{array} Part A. The smooth surface. Pick a point (a,b). Then: Compute f(a,b),\qquad f_x(a,b),\qquad f_y(a,b). Write the linear approximation  oxed{ L(x,y)=f(a,b)+f_x(a,b)(x-a)+f_y(a,b)(y-b). } Plot the surface z=f(x,y) and the tangent plane z=L(x,y) on the same axes. Choose several nearby displacement vectors \mathbf h=(h,k). For each one, compute the error E(\mathbf h)=f(a+h,b+k)-L(a+h,b+k). Make a table containing \|\mathbf h\|, \qquad |E(\mathbf h)|, \qquad  rac{|E(\mathbf h)|}{\|\mathbf h\|}, \qquad  rac{|E(\mathbf h)|}{\|\mathbf h\|^2}. The first ratio should get small as \|\mathbf h\|	o0. That is the signature of differentiability. Part B. The nonsmooth surface. At the origin, draw or plot z=\sqrt{x^2+y^2} or z=|x|+|y|. Then answer: Does the surface look like it has one tangent plane at the origin? What do the slices along the x-axis and y-axis look like? Why is a tangent plane not the same thing as matching a few coordinate slices? How does the picture explain the warning from : partial information in some directions does not guarantee a true linear approximation in all directions? What to hand in. Your report should include: the smooth surface and point you chose, the tangent plane calculation, one plot or hand sketch showing the surface and tangent plane together, an error table, one plot or hand sketch of the nonsmooth surface, a paragraph explaining why the smooth surface has a local linear model and the nonsmooth surface does not. The main lesson is visual: differentiability means that the graph becomes flatter and flatter under a microscope. A corner, cusp, or cone tip refuses to become one plane.

Project 2: moments of inertia of real objects Mass tells how much material an object has. Moment of inertia tells how that mass is spread around an axis. A heavy object is harder to spin if more of its mass is far from the axis. That is why a figure skater spins faster when pulling in her arms. The mass has not changed, but the mass has moved closer to the rotation axis. The integral measures this spreading.  oxed{ I=\int (	ext{distance to axis})^2\,dm. } For a thin plate with density \delta(x,y), this becomes  oxed{ I=\iint_R (	ext{distance to axis})^2\,\delta(x,y)\,dA. } A seed example: a rectangular tray. A rectangular tray is 4 meters long and 2 meters wide. Put its center at the origin, so the tray occupies -2\le x\le2, \qquad -1\le y\le1. Suppose the tray has constant areal density \delta=0.5	ext{ kg/m}^2. Find the moment of inertia about the vertical axis through its center. The distance from the point (x,y) to the vertical axis is r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}. So r^2=x^2+y^2. Thus  egin{aligned} I_z \amp= \iint_R (x^2+y^2)\delta\,dA\ \amp= 0.5\int_{-2}^{2}\int_{-1}^{1}(x^2+y^2)\,dy\,dx. \end{aligned} Separate the integral:  egin{aligned} I_z \amp= 0.5\left( \int_{-2}^{2}\int_{-1}^{1}x^2\,dy\,dx + \int_{-2}^{2}\int_{-1}^{1}y^2\,dy\,dx ight)\ \amp= 0.5\left( \int_{-2}^{2}2x^2\,dx + \int_{-2}^{2} rac{2}{3}\,dx ight)\ \amp= 0.5\left( 2\cdot rac{16}{3} +  rac{2}{3}\cdot4 ight)\ \amp= 0.5\left(  rac{32}{3}+ rac{8}{3} ight)\ \amp=  rac{20}{3}. \end{aligned} So  oxed{ I_z= rac{20}{3}	ext{ kg}\cdot	ext{m}^2. } The tray's mass is M=0.5(4)(2)=4	ext{ kg}. The radius of gyration is the distance R_g that would give the same moment of inertia if all the mass were concentrated on a circle: I_z=MR_g^2. Thus R_g=\sqrt{ rac{I_z}{M}} = \sqrt{ rac{20/3}{4}} = \sqrt{ rac53}. This number is not the physical radius of the tray. It is a weighted average distance from the axis. Mass farther from the axis contributes more to moment of inertia.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85, >=stealth] \draw[->] (-2.8,0) -- (2.9,0) node[right] {\(x\)}; \draw[->] (0,-1.8) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {\(y\)}; \draw[fill=gray!10, thick] (-2,-1) rectangle (2,1); \draw[very thick, blue!65!black, fill=white] (0,0) circle (0.12);  ill[blue!65!black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt); 
ode[blue!65!black, align=center] at (1.45,1.55) {\(z\)-axis perpendicular\to the plate};  ill (1.35,0.65) circle (2pt); \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (1.35,0.65); 
ode[above right] at (0.7,0.35) {\(r\)}; 
ode at (0,-2.35) {moment of inertia weights each mass piece by \(r^2\)}; \end{tikzpicture} Your task. Choose one object or model. Option A. Uniform rectangular plate. Compute the moment of inertia of a rectangular plate about one of its symmetry axes and about the axis perpendicular to the plate through its center. Option B. Disk or annulus. Use polar coordinates to compute the moment of inertia of a uniform disk or annulus about its central axis. Option C. Triangular lamina. Choose a triangular region in the plane. Compute its mass and moment of inertia using double integrals. Option D. Measured object. Approximate a real flat object by dividing it into small pieces. Estimate each piece's mass and distance from the chosen axis. Use the discrete approximation I\approx \sum r_i^2\,\Delta m_i. Option E. Solid object. Compute or estimate the moment of inertia of a solid cylinder, rectangular box, or ball using a triple integral. Required steps. Draw the object and the rotation axis. State whether the density is constant or variable. Write the distance squared to the axis. Set up the integral: I=\iint_R r^2\delta\,dA or I=\iiint_E r^2ho\,dV. Choose coordinates that make the integral simpler. Compute the mass. Compute the moment of inertia. Interpret the answer physically. Questions to answer. Which parts of the object contribute most to I? What happens if the same mass is moved farther from the axis? Does symmetry simplify the integral? Derive a useful check directly from the integral definition. For a plate in the xy-plane, I_z=I_x+I_y because x^2+y^2 is the sum of the squared distances to the two in-plane axes. For parallel axes a distance d apart, one through the center of mass, expanding the squared distance gives I=I_{\mathrm{cm}}+Md^2; the linear terms vanish by the definition of center of mass. Verify your answer with either identity where applicable. How would the answer change if the density were larger near the edge? What to hand in. Your report should include: a labeled diagram, the density model, the chosen axis, the moment-of-inertia integral, the calculation, the final value with units, a physical explanation of why the answer is reasonable. Moment of inertia is an integral with a memory of distance. A mass piece does not only count by how much mass it has. It counts by where it sits.

Project 3: change of variables in image warping A change of variables is a controlled distortion. In , a coordinate map sent tiny squares in one coordinate plane to tiny parallelograms in another. The determinant measured how the area changed. The same idea appears in image warping. A square image may be stretched, sheared, wrapped, or bent. If we want to compute area, brightness, or ink after the warp, the Jacobian must be included. A seed example: a square stretched into a trapezoid. Start with the unit square 0\le u\le1, \qquad 0\le v\le1. Define a map T(u,v)=(x,y)= igl(u,\,(1+u)v igr). When u=0, the vertical height is 1. When u=1, the vertical height is 2. So the square is warped into a trapezoid. The tangent vectors are T_u=(1,v), \qquad T_v=(0,1+u). The Jacobian determinant is  egin{aligned} J(u,v) \amp= \det  egin{pmatrix} x_u \amp x_v\ y_u \amp y_v \end{pmatrix} \ \amp= \det  egin{pmatrix} 1\amp0\ v\amp1+u \end{pmatrix}\ \amp= 1+u. \end{aligned} So a tiny square of area du\,dv becomes a tiny region of area approximately (1+u)\,du\,dv. The area of the warped image is  egin{aligned} \operatorname{Area} \amp= \int_0^1\int_0^1 (1+u)\,dv\,du\ \amp= \int_0^1(1+u)\,du\ \amp=  rac32. \end{aligned} That matches the ordinary trapezoid formula:  rac12(1+2)(1)= rac32. Now suppose the brightness of the warped image is B(x,y)=x+y. Then the total brightness is \iint_{	ext{warped image}} B(x,y)\,dA. Using the change of variables,  egin{aligned} \iint_{	ext{warped image}} B\,dA \amp= \int_0^1\int_0^1 B(T(u,v))\,|J(u,v)|\,dv\,du\ \amp= \int_0^1\int_0^1  igl(u+(1+u)v igr)(1+u)\,dv\,du. \end{aligned} Compute the inner integral:  egin{aligned} \int_0^1  igl(u+(1+u)v igr)(1+u)\,dv \amp= u(1+u)+ rac12(1+u)^2. \end{aligned} Then  egin{aligned} \int_0^1 \left[ u(1+u)+ rac12(1+u)^2 ight]du \amp= \int_0^1 \left(  rac12+2u+ rac32u^2 ight)du\ \amp=  rac12+1+ rac12\ \amp= 2. \end{aligned} So  oxed{ \iint_{	ext{warped image}}(x+y)\,dA=2. } A coordinate warp changes the area of small grid cells. The Jacobian records the local area scale.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85, >=stealth]  egin{scope} \draw[->] (-0.3,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {\(u\)}; \draw[->] (0,-0.3) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {\(v\)}; \draw[fill=gray!10, thick] (0,0) rectangle (1.5,1.5);  oreach \x in {0.3,0.6,0.9,1.2} { \draw[gray!45] (\x,0) -- (\x,1.5); }  oreach \y in {0.3,0.6,0.9,1.2} { \draw[gray!45] (0,\y) -- (1.5,\y); } 
