Project 3: Green's theorem and area from GPS boundary data A region's area can be found without filling the region with a grid. It can be found by walking around the boundary. Green's theorem explains why. Let \eta= rac12(x\,dy-y\,dx). Then d\eta \amp =  rac12(dx\wedge dy-dy\wedge dx) \amp = dx\wedge dy. So, for a positively oriented boundary,  oxed{ \operatorname{Area}(R) = \oint_{\partial R} rac12(x\,dy-y\,dx). } This formula is the continuous version of the shoelace formula for polygons. A seed example: four boundary points. Suppose a surveyor records four boundary points of a small field, measured in meters from a local reference point:  egin{array}{c|cc} 	ext{Point} \amp x \amp y \ \hline P_0 \amp 0 \amp 0\ P_1 \amp 5 \amp 1\ P_2 \amp 4 \amp 4\ P_3 \amp 1 \amp 3 \end{array} Assume the points are listed counterclockwise and connected by straight segments. On a line segment from (x_i,y_i) to (x_{i+1},y_{i+1}), the integral of \eta is  oxed{  rac12(x_i y_{i+1}-y_i x_{i+1}). } Therefore \operatorname{Area} \amp =  rac12\sum_{i=0}^{3} (x_i y_{i+1}-y_i x_{i+1}) \amp =  rac12\Bigl[ (0\cdot1-0\cdot5) + (5\cdot4-1\cdot4) \amp \qquad + (4\cdot3-4\cdot1) + (1\cdot0-3\cdot0) \Bigr] \amp =  rac12(0+16+8+0) \amp = 12. So the field has area  oxed{ 12	ext{ m}^2. } If the points were listed clockwise, the same calculation would give -12. The negative sign would not mean negative physical area. It would mean the boundary orientation was reversed. Green's theorem turns a boundary walk into an area calculation.  egin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75, >=stealth] \draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (6.2,0) node[right] {\(x\)}; \draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,5.2) node[above] {\(y\)}; \draw[gray!20, step=1] (0,0) grid (6,5); \draw[fill=green!10, thick] (0,0) -- (5,1) -- (4,4) -- (1,3) -- cycle; \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (0,0) -- (2.3,0.46); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (5,1) -- (4.55,2.35); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (4,4) -- (2.55,3.55); \draw[->, very thick, red!65!black] (1,3) -- (0.4